@* P0-6 (translation audit) — the client-JS strings bundle: the closed
   KnownTranslationKeys set resolved in the effective language, serialized
   to JSON. The client modules (rich-editor / image-editor / tag-suggest /
   notifications-bell / kiosk / note-modal / onboarding-modal) read this
   once and localize their modals, labels and alerts (with the en floor
   as the in-code fallback).

   Must be emitted via Html.Raw so the JSON arrives unencoded (a bare
   @expr inside a <script> IS HTML-encoded in .NET 10's Razor — the `"`
   becomes &quot; and JSON.parse fails). The only hazard is a value
   containing a literal `</script>` closing the element early, so
   neutralize `</` to `<\/` (a valid JSON escape that cannot form
   `</script>`). The previous form (HttpUtility.HtmlEncode) double-encoded
   the block (&amp;quot;), making the entire P0-6 bundle unparseable —
   the client modules silently fell back to the en floor strings. *@
<script type="application/json" id="kumunita-strings">@Html.Raw(Model.Replace("</", "<\\/"))</script>
